  <article xml:id="session5wks">
    <title>MATH 152 Hands-On, Grades Up</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        For workspace between exercises, print this worksheet or download to your device by clicking the printer icon.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <worksheet>
      <title>Session #5: Trig Substitution, Partial Fractions &amp; Improper Integrals -- Sections 7.3, 7.4 &amp; 7.8</title>

      <!-- PAGE 1: Warm-up problems 1-2 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Warm-Up</title>
          <p>Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts with peers around you.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>Circle the integrals below that are improper.</p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="3%">
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{e^2} \frac{1}{\ln(x)}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{1} \frac{1}{x^2 - 4}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{3} \frac{1}{x^2 + 2}\,dx</m></p>
            </sidebyside>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="3%">
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{2}^{e^2} \frac{1}{\ln(x)}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-3}^{1} \frac{1}{x^2 - 4}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-2}^{\infty} \frac{1}{x^2 + 2}\,dx</m></p>
            </sidebyside>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="3%">
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{\frac{1}{e}}^{e^2} \frac{1}{\ln(x)}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{2} \frac{1}{x^2 - 4}\,dx</m></p>
              <p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-\sqrt{2}}^{2} \frac{1}{x^2 + 2}\,dx</m></p>
            </sidebyside>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
          <introduction>
            <p>For each of the fractions below, write out the form of the partial fraction decomposition. <alert>For this problem, you do not need to determine the numerical values of the coefficients.</alert></p>
            <p>Example: <m>\dfrac{3 + x}{(x+1)(x+2)} = \dfrac{A}{x+1} + \dfrac{B}{x+2}</m></p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="1.1in">
            <statement><p><m>\dfrac{x^2 + 2x - 1}{2x^3 - 7x^2 + 3x}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="1.1in">
            <statement><p><m>\dfrac{2x^3 - x^2 - 3x + 4}{x^2 - 2x + 1}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 2: Warm-up problem 3 (trig substitution table) -->
      <page>
        <exercise>
          <statement>
            <p><alert>Directions</alert>: Complete the rest of the table below to help you solve integrals using trigonometric substitution. The first row is done for you.</p>
            <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
              <col width="22%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="28%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="48%" right="minor"/>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>For integrals of the form...</cell>
                <cell>Substitute...</cell>
                <cell>To obtain...</cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{a^2 - u^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>u = a\sin(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>and use the identity</p>
                  <p><m>1 - \sin^2(\theta) = \cos^2(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>for <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \leq \theta \leq \frac{\pi}{2}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{a^2 - u^2}\,d\theta = \int \sqrt{a^2 - (a\sin(\theta))^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle = \int \sqrt{a^2 - a^2\sin^2(\theta)}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle = \int \sqrt{a^2(1 - \sin^2(\theta))}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle = \int \sqrt{a^2\cos^2(\theta)}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle = \int a\cos(\theta)\,d\theta</m></p>
                </cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{a^2 + u^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>u = a\tan(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>and use the identity</p>
                  <p><m>1 + \tan^2(\theta) = \sec^2(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>for <m>-\frac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{a^2 + u^2}\,d\theta = \int \sqrt{a^2 + (a\tan(\theta))^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                </cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{u^2 - a^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>u = a\sec(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>and use the identity</p>
                  <p><m>\sec^2(\theta) - 1 = \tan^2(\theta)</m></p>
                  <p>for <m>0 \leq \theta \lt \frac{\pi}{2}</m></p>
                  <p>or</p>
                  <p>for <m>\pi \leq \theta \lt \frac{3\pi}{2}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell>
                  <p><m>\displaystyle\int \sqrt{u^2 - a^2}\,d\theta = \int \sqrt{(a\sec(\theta))^2 - a^2}\,d\theta</m></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                  <p><nbsp/></p>
                </cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 3: Hands-on problem 1 (circle the correct step) -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Hands-on</title>
          <p>Answer each of the following as best you can. Feel free to discuss your thoughts with peers around you.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise number="1">
          <statement>
            <p>The steps for calculating <m>\displaystyle\int_{-3}^{-1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 + 4x + 5}}\,dx</m> are in the correct order. However, at each step, you are presented with two choices: a correct expression and an incorrect expression. For each step, circle the correct expression/equation and provide a brief explanation of what is wrong with the incorrect expression/equation.</p>
            <tabular halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
              <col width="9%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="34%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="34%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="21%" right="minor"/>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Step</cell>
                <cell>Choice 1</cell>
                <cell>Choice 2</cell>
                <cell>Why incorrect?</cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 1</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\sqrt{x^2 + 4x + 5} = \sqrt{(x+2)^2 + 1^2}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\sqrt{x^2 + 4x + 5} = \sqrt{(x+4)(x+1)}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 2</p></cell>
                <cell><p>Substitute <m>x + 2 = \tan(\theta)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p>Substitute <m>x = 2\tan(\theta)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 3</p></cell>
                <cell><p>Substitute <m>dx = 2\sec^2(\theta)\,d\theta</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p>Substitute <m>dx = \sec^2(\theta)\,d\theta</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 4</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\int_{-3}^{-1} \frac{\sec^2(\theta)\,d\theta}{\sqrt{\tan^2(\theta) + 1}}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{\sec^2(\theta)\,d\theta}{\sqrt{\tan^2(\theta) + 1}}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 5</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \tfrac{1}{2}\sec(\theta)\,d\theta</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \sec(\theta)\,d\theta</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 6</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\ln\left|\sec(\theta)\right|\Big|_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\ln\left|\sec(\theta) + \tan(\theta)\right|\Big|_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4}</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 7</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\ln\left(\sqrt{2} + 1\right) - \ln\left(\sqrt{2} - 1\right)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\ln\left(\sqrt{2} - 1\right) - \ln\left(\sqrt{2} + 1\right)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell><p># 8</p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>\ln\left(2\sqrt{2}\right)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><m>2\ln\left(1 + \sqrt{2}\right)</m></p></cell>
                <cell><p><nbsp/></p></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 4: Hands-on problem 2 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="2">
          <introduction>
            <p>Compute the following integrals:</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3.7in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2 - 1}}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3.7in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int \frac{\sqrt{9 - x^2}}{x^2}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 5: Hands-on problem 3 (shuffled steps table) -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="3">
          <statement>
            <p>The steps for computing the definite integral <m>\int_{1}^{2} \frac{(x-1)^3}{x^3 + x^2}\,dx</m>, have been shuffled in the table below. Number the steps with the numbers 1 through 10, indicating the order in which the equations would appear had someone correctly calculated this integral using partial fractions.</p>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor" right="minor">
              <col width="76%" right="minor"/>
              <col width="22%" right="minor"/>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Re-arrange terms</alert> <nbsp/> <m>-4x^2 + 3x - 1 = (A + C)x^2 + (A + B)x + B</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Simplify</alert> <nbsp/> <m>\frac{1}{2} + 12\ln(2) - 8\ln(3)</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Polynomial long division</alert> <nbsp/> <m>\dfrac{(x-1)^3}{x^3 + x^2} = 1 + \dfrac{-4x^2 + 3x - 1}{x^2(x+1)}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Rewrite integral</alert> <nbsp/> <m>\int_{1}^{2} \frac{(x-1)^3}{x^3 + x^2}\,dx = \int_{1}^{2}\left[1 + \frac{4}{x} - \frac{1}{x^2} - \frac{8}{x+1}\right]dx</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Evaluate at endpoints</alert> <nbsp/> <m>2 + 4\ln(2) + \frac{1}{2} - 8\ln(3) - 1 - 4\ln(1) - \frac{1}{1} + 8\ln(2)</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Create linear system of equations</alert> <nbsp/> <m>A + C = -4</m></p>
                  <p><m>A + B = 3</m></p>
                  <p><m>B = -1</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Multiply by Denominator</alert> <nbsp/> <m>-4x^2 + 3x - 1 = Ax(x+1) + B(x+1) + Cx^2</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Solve system of equations for coefficients</alert> <nbsp/> <m>A = 4,\ B = -1,\ C = -8</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Find antiderivatives</alert> <nbsp/> <m>\left[x + 4\ln(x) + \frac{1}{x} - 8\ln(x+1)\right]\Bigg|_{1}^{2}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
              <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                  <p><alert>Write out partial fractions</alert> <nbsp/> <m>\dfrac{-4x^2 + 3x - 1}{x^2(x+1)} = \dfrac{A}{x} + \dfrac{B}{x^2} + \dfrac{C}{x+1}</m></p>
                </cell>
                <cell><p>Step # <fillin characters="5"/></p></cell>
              </row>
            </tabular>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 6: Hands-on problem 4 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="4">
          <introduction>
            <p>Compute the following integrals using partial fraction decomposition:</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3.7in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int \frac{3}{(t+2)^2(t-2)}\,dt</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3.7in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int \frac{x^2 + 2x - 1}{2x^3 - 7x^2 + 3x}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 7: Hands-on problem 5 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="5">
          <introduction>
            <p>Determine whether the following improper integrals converge or diverge.</p>
            <p>If it converges, find the value of the integral.</p>
            <p>If it diverges, explain why.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="2.3in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{2}^{\infty} \frac{x}{x^2 + 1}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.3in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{0} x e^x\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="2.3in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-\infty}^{0} \frac{6}{x^2 + 9}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 8: Hands-on problem 6 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="6">
          <introduction>
            <p>Determine whether the following improper integrals converge or diverge.</p>
            <p>If it converges, find the value of the integral.</p>
            <p>If it diverges, explain why.</p>
          </introduction>
          <task workspace="3.5in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{-1}^{2} \frac{1}{(2x - 1)}\,dx</m></p></statement>
          </task>
          <task workspace="3.5in">
            <statement><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{0}^{5} \frac{dx}{25 - x^2}</m></p></statement>
          </task>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 9: Comparison Theorem + Hands-on problems 7-8 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Comparison Theorem</title>
          <p>Suppose that <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are continuous functions with <m>f(x) \geq g(x) \geq 0</m> for <m>x \geq a</m>.</p>
          <p>(a) If <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{\infty} f(x)\,dx</m> is convergent, then <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{\infty} g(x)\,dx</m> is convergent.</p>
          <p>(b) If <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{\infty} g(x)\,dx</m> is divergent, then <m>\displaystyle\int_{a}^{\infty} f(x)\,dx</m> is divergent.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise number="7" workspace="3.2in">
          <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the improper integral
              <me>\int_{2}^{\infty} \frac{\cos^2(x)}{x^2}\,dx</me>
              converges or diverges using the Comparison Theorem.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
        <exercise number="8" workspace="3.2in">
          <statement>
            <p>Determine whether the improper integral:
              <me>\int_{1}^{\infty} \frac{\sin(6x) + 8}{x^4 + \sqrt{x}}\,dx</me>
              converges or diverges using the Comparison Theorem.</p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 10: Grades Up! problems 1-2 -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Grades Up!</title>
          <p>Choose the best answer for each of the following.</p>
        </assemblage>

        <exercise number="1">
          <statement>
            <p>Evaluate the definite integral:
              <me>\int_{0}^{2} \frac{1}{(x^2 + 4)^{3/2}}\,dx</me>
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{1}{8}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{4}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{1}{4}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{8}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{1}{4}\sin(2)</m></p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise number="2">
          <statement>
            <p>The improper integral
              <me>\int_{0}^{1} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}\,dx</me>
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p>Diverges to <m>\infty</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Converges to 1</p></li>
                <li><p>Converges to 2</p></li>
                <li><p>Converges to <m>\dfrac{1}{2}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>Diverges to <m>-\infty</m></p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 11: Grades Up! problems 3-4 -->
      <page>
        <exercise number="3">
          <statement>
            <p>Rewrite the integrand into a form suitable for integration:
              <me>\int \frac{2x + 3}{(x-1)(x^2+1)}\,dx</me>
              Which of the following is the correct partial fraction decomposition?
              <ol marker="(a)">
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{2x+3}{(x-1)(x^2+1)} = \dfrac{A}{x-1} + \dfrac{Bx + C}{(x+1)^2}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{2x+3}{(x-1)(x^2+1)} = \dfrac{A}{x-1} + \dfrac{Bx + C}{x^2 + 1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{2x+3}{(x-1)(x^2+1)} = \dfrac{Ax + B}{x-1} + \dfrac{C}{x^2 + 1}</m></p></li>
                <li><p><m>\dfrac{2x+3}{(x-1)(x^2+1)} = \dfrac{A}{x-1} + \dfrac{B}{x^2 + 1} + \dfrac{C}{(x^2+1)^2}</m></p></li>
                <li><p>None of these.</p></li>
              </ol>
            </p>
            <p><nbsp/></p>
          </statement>
        </exercise>

        <exercise number="4">
          <statement>
            <p>If <m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} f(x)\,dx</m> diverges, which of the following statements is correct:</p>
            <sidebyside widths="55% 38%" margins="0% 7%" valign="middle">
              <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                  <li><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} g(x)</m> diverges by comparison with <m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                  <li><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} h(x)</m> converges by comparison with <m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                  <li><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} g(x)</m> converges by comparison with <m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                  <li><p><m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} h(x)</m> diverges by comparison with <m>\displaystyle\int_{1}^{\infty} f(x)</m>.</p></li>
                  <li><p>both (a) and (d) are correct.</p></li>
                </ol>
              </p>
              <image source="s5-comparison-curves.jpg">
                <description>Graph of three positive decreasing curves for x greater than or equal to 1: g of x lies on top, f of x in the middle, and h of x on the bottom, with all three approaching the x-axis as x grows.</description>
              </image>
            </sidebyside>
          </statement>
        </exercise>
      </page>

      <!-- PAGE 12: Challenge -->
      <page>
        <assemblage>
          <title>Challenge</title>
          <p>The parabola <m>y = x^2</m> divides the disk <m>x^2 + y^2 \leq 2</m> into two parts. Find the areas of both parts.</p>
        </assemblage>
      </page>

    </worksheet>
  </article>

</pretext>
